A key element of the exercises is the semester-long project work.

Students are divided into groups of 2-4 and work on the same project throughout the semester, giving them time to thoroughly familiarize themselves with the problem to be solved. At the end of the semester, the submitted project work forms the basis for grading.

## Requirements

Both the practical signature and the course grade depend on the project work.

### Requirements for the signature

The requirements for obtaining a practical signature are as follows
- Weekly progress in the group's GitHub repository.
- The group's GitHub repository contains all [documentation to be produced](#documentation-to-be-produced) by the end of the semester.

The above is supplemented by the known requirement regarding absences: students may miss a maximum of three practical sessions.
{% linkcard title="GitHub"
   href="https://github.com/unideb-advanced-software-engineering"
   description="Jump to the GitHub organization containing the group repositories."
   /%}

### The course grade

Team members receive their course grades for the oral defense of the documentation submitted as part of the project work.

**The oral defense will take place on Tuesday, May 19, 2026, at 4:00 p.m. in Room IK-201. An exam session for your project defense has been posted in the Neptun system, and you need to register for it.**

The defense will proceed as follows:

- The committee members will review the teams' submitted work in advance (presumably over the weekend).
- Teams will present their work in 7 minutes.
  - The format and tools used for the presentation are at the team's discretion.
  - It is not mandatory for every team member to present; it is acceptable for a single member to deliver the presentation.
  - The 7-minute time limit is strict; therefore, we recommend that teams practice their presentation in advance.
- The committee will ask the team questions for 7 minutes.
  - Any team member may answer the questions.
  - The 7-minute time limit is strictly enforced here as well.
- The committee evaluates the team's work based on the submitted project, the presentation, and the answers to the questions.

{% aside type="tip" %}
The presentation should primarily address the following questions:
- Why were those architectural characteristics chosen?
- Regarding issue X, why did we decide this way and not another way?
- Which requirements posed the greatest challenges, and why?
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="tip" %}
The committee's questions may also include “what if?” scenarios, such as:

> If we were to change requirement X in a certain way, how would that affect the architecture?

We can also expect questions related to technology:

> What technology would we use to implement component X?
{% /aside %}

With regard to the presentation and the questions, it is important to note that the committee is primarily interested in the team’s ability to present a case. Rather than the actual decisions themselves, what matters is how well the team can justify and defend them.

## Documentation to be produced

A project is considered complete if it contains **all** of the documentation elements listed below.

Documentation elements are **only** considered submitted if they are available on the `main` branch of the group's GitHub repository.

### General Requirements

All submitted documents must meet the following requirements:

- The submitted documentation shoud be in English and You should ensure grammatical correctness and proper spelling.
- Ensure that everything (whether a requirement, quality attribute, architectural characteristic, or architectural decision) can be clearly traced back to the original case study. In other words, almost every choice and decision ultimately stems from the original case study. The only exceptions are decisions based on the team’s experience and preferences (“We use JavaScript because we have extensive experience with it.”).

We will compile the resulting documents into a publicly accessible Astro documentation site (potentially hosted via GitHub Pages), which should be:
- easy and logical to navigate,
- aesthetically pleasing.

In addition to the required elements, the site may include optional content (related to the case study and the subject).

{% aside type="caution" title="Attention" %}

There is no single perfect solution for any of the case studies. There is no answer key against which to evaluate the submitted work.

The basis for the evaluation is not your decisions themselves, but the context in which they were made: what options you considered and, above all, why you arrived at that particular decision.

{% /aside %}

### Software Requirements Specification

Based on the case study description, students derive specific requirements, which they use to fill in the appropriate sections of the [IEEE SRS template](/site/srs-template.doc).

Criteria for evaluating the SRS:
- The document clearly states what problem the product solves, what functions it provides, and who the users are.
- The relationship between the software and the external environment is described: the external systems used and the various interfaces.
- All major functionalities of the software are described in detail: who does what, why, and how.
- Non-functional requirements and quality attribute scenarios related to the functionality are included.

### Architectural Characteristics

A list of the qualitative characteristics that define the architecture of the application to be developed. In other words, a compilation of architectural characteristics.

Each characteristic must be accompanied by an explanation of why it is considered an architectural characteristic.

### Architecturally Significant Requirements

A list of requirements that define the architecture of the application to be developed.

Each requirement must be accompanied by an explanation of why it qualifies as an ASR.

### Architecture Style

At least one, but likely more than one, selected architectural style.

Different subsystems may require different styles, and in the case of distributed systems, the architecture of individual components may be relevant.

The selected architectural style(s) must be documented in an ADR.

### Architectural Decision Record

**At least 3** (*5 or more recommended*) ADRs in any style (Y-Statement, Nygard, MADR, etc.), each of which documents an architecturally significant decision.

What kinds of decisions might these be? Here are a few examples (in addition to documenting the styles):
- Breaking down the system into subsystems and components. Whether you used ADD or not, your system will inevitably have smaller components. Countless ADRs can be created for this breakdown.
- Communication style and implementation between components.
- Data storage considerations.
- Selected technologies.

### C4 Diagram

Create the following diagrams for the software:

- C4 System Context diagram,
- C4 Container diagram.

The diagrams should be:
- informative (take full advantage of the capabilities provided by LikeC4),
- consistent (for example, label the same thing in the same way),
- aesthetically pleasing.